            <div xml:id="d337">

                <pb facs="0466" n="372" xml:id="pg372"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.5048/347: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Liberia</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Reber</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-11-17T15:00:00-05:00">November 17, 1930—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>103. Department’s telegram No. 100, November 13, 1 p.m. I received the Liberian
                    Consul General at noon today and handed him a memorandum which I requested he
                    communicate without delay to the President of Liberia.</p>
                <p>[Here follow summary and extracts from the memorandum, printed on <ref
                        target="#pg369">page 369</ref>.]</p>
                <p>Should President King question you with regard to the memorandum, I believe it
                    would be desirable to make no comment other than the statement that you had been
                    informed of its delivery by your Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
